     "text": "Creating a Vampire (GM/NPC villain, or PC by group agreement): same starting stats as a wizard, same item slots minus armour/shields. No apprentices (may nominate a warband member as one per game); 9 soldiers (4 specialist). School \"Vampire\" — Aligned (+2): Chronomancer, Necromancer, Soothsayer; Neutral (+4): Elementalist, Enchanter, Illusionist, Sigilist, Summoner, Witch; Antithetical: Thaumaturge (never learnable). Starts with 8 spells: 3 Vampire, 1 per aligned school, 2 from 2 neutral schools. Traits: Undead, Immune to Control Undead, Magic Attacks, Mind Lock, Thaumaturgic Vulnerability, True Sight, Partial Immunity to Normal Damage. New levels cost 120xp (max 300xp/game); Will capped at +5; Health cap 22; can't learn/cast Transcendence; can't benefit from Heal/Miraculous Cure/Healing Potions; Animal Companion yields an undead animal; never has Rangifers; regains Health equal to damage dealt to giants in melee. Unnatural Health: no natural post-game healing — pay 5gc per Health point regained, or consume a permanent (non-undead/construct/demon) warband member's blood instead (removes them from the roster, replaceable next game; vampire runs at 8 soldiers that game); failing to pay at 0 Health counts as a Dead result. Sun Damage: 1 damage per activation in uncovered sunlight. Becoming a Vampire (usually accidental): gains all vampire traits/rules, loses Thaumaturge spells (all other Casting Numbers +1), Will capped at +5, level recalculated down for lost/degraded spells and Will, school becomes Vampire, loses apprentice (gains a 9th soldier slot instead). A card-deck version of the 8 Vampire spells (\"Vampire Spell Cards\") appears in the book's appendix, identical in effect. Running Creature Spellcasters (GM technique, reusable for any monster spellcaster): Recruit Another Player to run it; Fight and Switch (play the scenario twice, swapping sides); Shared Control (control passes between players, e.g. per-turn or by secret bid); or an Instruction 